============================================================
CPDMA DESCRIPTOR hw_mode — COMPLETE DETAILED EXPLANATION
============================================================


============================================================
1) hw_mode — EXACT MEANING AND BIT LAYOUT
============================================================

--- Definition
    In CPDMA, hw_mode is the MAIN CONTROL WORD of a DMA descriptor.
    It tells the DMA engine HOW to interpret this descriptor.

--- Conceptual layout
    31                     16 15            0
    +----------------------+-----------------+
    |     control bits     |     length      |
    +----------------------+-----------------+

    - Upper bits  : Control / flags
    - Lower bits  : Buffer length (bytes)


============================================================
2) TYPICAL CPDMA hw_mode BITS (TX)
============================================================

--- Bit layout (TX path)

    Bit   Name        Meaning
    ----------------------------------------------
    31    OWNER       1 = owned by DMA
                      0 = owned by CPU

    30    EOQ         End Of Queue (set by DMA)

    29    SOP         Start Of Packet

    28    EOP         End Of Packet

    27    PASS_CRC    Hardware appends Ethernet CRC

    26:24 PORT        Destination port (directed TX)

    15:0  LENGTH      Number of bytes in this buffer


============================================================
3) HOW DRIVER BUILDS hw_mode
============================================================

--- Code example
    mode = CPDMA_DESC_OWNER |
           CPDMA_DESC_SOP   |
           CPDMA_DESC_EOP;

    writel_relaxed(mode | len, &desc->hw_mode);

--- What this means
    - Sets DMA ownership
    - Marks this descriptor as both
        * Start Of Packet
        * End Of Packet
    - Encodes buffer length in lower bits

--- Result
    This descriptor represents ONE COMPLETE PACKET.


============================================================
4) MEANING OF IMPORTANT hw_mode BITS
============================================================

------------------------------------------------------------
4.1 OWNER (CPDMA_DESC_OWNER)
------------------------------------------------------------

--- Meaning
    Ownership handoff mechanism between CPU and DMA

--- Values
    0 → CPU owns descriptor
        - Safe for driver to modify

    1 → DMA owns descriptor
        - Hardware may read/write
        - CPU MUST NOT touch descriptor

--- Lifecycle
    CPU sets OWNER → DMA processes → DMA clears OWNER


------------------------------------------------------------
4.2 SOP (Start Of Packet)
------------------------------------------------------------

--- Meaning
    Indicates this descriptor contains
    the FIRST byte of a packet.

--- Used by DMA to
    - Start a new frame assembly
    - Reset internal packet state


------------------------------------------------------------
4.3 EOP (End Of Packet)
------------------------------------------------------------

--- Meaning
    Indicates this descriptor contains
    the LAST byte of a packet.

--- Used by DMA to
    - Transmit the assembled frame
    - Generate TX completion


------------------------------------------------------------
4.4 PORT (Directed TX)
------------------------------------------------------------

--- Purpose
    Tells CPSW which port to transmit on.

--- Encoding
    Inserted via:
        cpdma_desc_to_port(chan, mode, si->directed);

--- Examples
    PORT = EMAC0
    PORT = EMAC1
    PORT = HOST

--- Without PORT
    CPSW would not know
    where to send the packet.


------------------------------------------------------------
4.5 LENGTH
------------------------------------------------------------

--- Location
    Lower 16 bits of hw_mode

--- Meaning
    Number of bytes DMA should read
    from hw_buffer for this descriptor

--- Encoding
    hw_mode = mode | len


============================================================
5) WHY SOP / EOP EXIST (MULTI-DESCRIPTOR PACKETS)
============================================================

--- Core problem
    Packets may NOT be contiguous in memory.

--- skb layout example
    skb->data       (64 bytes)
    skb->frag[0]    (512 bytes)
    skb->frag[1]    (300 bytes)

--- Requirement
    Hardware must support
    scatter-gather DMA.


============================================================
6) SINGLE-DESCRIPTOR PACKET (SIMPLE CASE)
============================================================

--- Descriptor flags
    SOP = 1
    EOP = 1

--- Meaning
    "This descriptor contains the entire packet."

--- Diagram
    Descriptor #1
    +-----------------------------+
    | SOP | EOP | buffer | length |
    +-----------------------------+

--- DMA behavior
    - Read buffer
    - Transmit packet
    - Done


============================================================
7) MULTI-DESCRIPTOR PACKET (SCATTER-GATHER)
============================================================

--- Descriptor chain layout

    Descriptor 1:
        SOP = 1
        EOP = 0
        (first fragment)

    Descriptor 2:
        SOP = 0
        EOP = 0
        (middle fragment)

    Descriptor 3:
        SOP = 0
        EOP = 1
        (last fragment)


--- ASCII view
    [ SOP ] ---> [ MID ] ---> [ EOP ]


--- Each descriptor contains
    - Its own hw_buffer
    - Its own LENGTH
    - hw_next pointer to next descriptor


============================================================
8) HOW CPDMA PROCESSES DESCRIPTOR CHAINS
============================================================

--- Processing logic
    - Sees SOP      → start new packet
    - Reads buffer1
    - Reads buffer2
    - Reads buffer3
    - Sees EOP      → transmit packet

--- From wire perspective
    ONE Ethernet frame is transmitted.


============================================================
9) WHY SOP / EOP ARE MANDATORY
============================================================

--- Without SOP / EOP
    Hardware would not know:
        - Where packet starts
        - Where packet ends

--- Consequences
    - Packets could be merged
    - Packets could be split incorrectly
    - Data corruption on the wire

--- Therefore
    SOP / EOP are REQUIRED flags.


============================================================
10) hw_next AND DESCRIPTOR LINKING
============================================================

--- Descriptor chaining
    desc1->hw_next = desc2;
    desc2->hw_next = desc3;
    desc3->hw_next = 0;

--- DMA behavior
    - Follows hw_next pointers
    - Stops when:
        * hw_next == 0
        * AND EOP was seen


============================================================
11) TX COMPLETION HANDLING
============================================================

--- After DMA finishes transmitting
    - DMA clears OWNER bit
    - DMA sets EOQ if needed
    - Interrupt is raised

--- Driver TX completion
    - Walks completed descriptors
    - Uses sw_token to identify skb
    - Frees skb ONLY after EOP descriptor

--- Important rule
    Packet is considered complete
    ONLY when the EOP descriptor completes.


============================================================
12) FINAL SUMMARY
============================================================

- hw_mode is the primary DMA control word
- OWNER controls CPU ↔ DMA ownership
- SOP / EOP define packet boundaries
- LENGTH defines bytes per descriptor
- hw_next enables scatter-gather DMA
- Multi-descriptor packets are normal
- Only EOP completes a packet
- Correct hw_mode flags are CRITICAL
  for correct packet transmission

